(vector
 (map
  (:adr/id "adr-2609112400-payload-v3-is-one-rewrite-and-one-decision")
  (:adr/title "Payload v3 is one rewrite and one decision")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-11")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609076000-kir-is-an-ipld-codec-schema-and-adl"))
  (:adr/body "
## The question

Root gap 02 asks whether payload v3 is possible and, if it is, what it
costs. The first two candidate measurements are already answered and
landed: all twelve normalize tags are representable, so there is no named
blocker waiting on io-ipld, and a moving DefCID reaches the language
conformance corpus and zero deployment locks.

This iteration measured the third thing, which is the one that sets the
price: what a converter would actually have to DO, dimension by dimension.

## Where it is measured

kotoba.kir-value-codec-differential-test in osaho. That namespace already
NAMED the three dimensions; what it did not say is what each one costs.
Three tests were added, one per dimension, rather than a parallel probe.

Run it:

    kbb -M:test -n kotoba.kir-value-codec-differential-test

## Dimension 1 -- tag alphabet. Mechanical.

A total mapping. Twelve normalize tags, twelve codes, plus one code the
codec has and normalize never emits (link). Nothing waits on io-ipld.

The test hands each side its OWN representation, which matters: an earlier
pass at this measurement handed normalize a bare platform float, read the
refusal as a missing capability, and nearly reported v3 blocked. Both
sides do refuse the other spelling, and the reason LITERALS are pinned
rather than the fact that something threw.

## Dimension 2 -- i64. NOT a formatting difference. This is the finding.

normalize states in its own comment that (i64 5) and 5 denote one value
and must share one identity, and it gives both the form the same tagged form.
The codec gives them code-integer and code-int64, and different bytes.

The two sides disagree about HOW MANY VALUES THERE ARE. A converter
cannot re-encode its way across that; it has to decide, and both answers
cost something:

  always emit code-integer      keeps the normalize invariant, but the
                                explicit i64 wrapper cannot round-trip and
                                values beyond the exact range have no code
  emit code-int64 when wrapped  round-trips, but SPLITS an identity that
                                is one today, so (i64 5) and 5 would get
                                different DefCIDs

This is the only non-mechanical part of payload v3. Everything else is a
rewrite; this is a decision about the value identity of the language.

## Dimension 3 -- map key order. Crosses, not merely differs.

The prior text says the orders differ. Different orders could still agree
on every key set that actually occurs, which would make the conversion
cheap. They do not agree.

Witness, one two-key map with keys aa and b:

    normalize  ->  aa then b     rank, then structural compare
    codec      ->  b then aa     shorter encoding first, then bytewise

So order cannot be carried through and every map with two or more keys
re-sorts. That is the mechanical cause of the frozen-vector result already
on the record: all ten disagree.

## The answer

v3 is possible. The cost is one rewrite and one decision.

The rewrite is dimensions 1 and 3 together, and it moves every DefCID --
which was already known from the frozen vectors and now has a named cause
rather than only a measurement.

The decision is dimension 2, and it is the only part a converter cannot
make on its own: whether (i64 5) and 5 remain one identity. Nothing in the
measurement settles it, because it is not a fact about the encoders.

## Seen red

Each of the three tests was broken through the SOURCE, not through its own
expected literals, and each failed naming the thing that was broken:

  tag the i64 wrapper separately   -> dimension 2 fails, showing the int
                                      tag against the new one, and the
                                      frozen goldens also move, which
                                      confirms the break reached DefCID
                                      assembly
  reverse the normalize map sort   -> dimension 3 fails on the witness
  rename the float refusal literal -> dimension 1 fails on the pinned
                                      literal, one assertion, and a
                                      control that only asked whether
                                      something threw would have passed

## A trap worth recording

The worktrees for this measurement could not be cut with origin/main.
west checkouts carry their remote under the ORG name, so the io-ipld
remote is kotoba-lang, not origin. The first attempt used origin/main for
every repository with stderr discarded, and io-ipld silently got no
worktree at all -- the loop reported success for osaho and said nothing
about the one that failed. It was caught only because the loop printed a
line per repository.

Resolve the remote per repository rather than assuming, and never discard
stderr on the step that creates the thing you are about to measure.

## What is not claimed

Nothing here promotes io-ipld from test-only to :deps. That is the v3
decision itself and it needs the dimension 2 answer first. The pin stays
where ADR-2609076000 decision 1 put it.
")))
